*Proof.* By the division algorithm, \( n = 3q + r \) with \( r \in \{0, 1, 2\} \). Then \( n + 1 = 3q + (r + 1) \) and \( n + 2 = 3q + (r + 2) \). The remainders of \( n, n + 1, n + 2 \) on division by 3 are therefore \( r \), \( r + 1 \), \( r + 2 \) reduced to \( \{0, 1, 2\} \), which are \( 0, 1, 2 \) in some order:
- \( r = 0 \): remainders \( 0, 1, 2 \);
- \( r = 1 \): remainders \( 1, 2, 0 \) (since \( n + 2 = 3(q + 1) \));
- \( r = 2 \): remainders \( 2, 0, 1 \) (since \( n + 1 = 3(q + 1) \), \( n + 2 = 3(q + 1) + 1 \)).

By uniqueness of the remainder, an integer is divisible by 3 exactly when its remainder is 0, which happens for exactly one of the three. \( \blacksquare \)
